  "rationale": "Multiple direct-match gout PRS candidates are present. PGS001789 is best supported overall because it is a genome-wide shrinkage score (PRS-CS-auto; 910,151 variants) developed in a large multi-biobank context (training cohorts listed across many biobanks) with a large validation sample size (n=359,345) and reports PRS-comparable signal (Nagelkerke's R2 with covariates regressed out = 0.031208) that is below the provided gout heritability ceiling (h2_liability=0.0525), making the metric plausible. Competing candidates often provide only full-model AUROC (not PRS-only), come from pan-trait/portability frameworks, or have unclear/unknown covariates; some report very high full-model AUROCs that could be covariate-driven and are not directly comparable. Main limitation: even for PGS001789, the discrimination metric shown (AUROC=0.807) is a full-model AUROC rather than an explicit PGS-only AUROC, and several other gout models exist with partially overlapping evidence, so the visible metadata do not uniquely identify a clearly superior single winner."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000611","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches for urinary bladder cancer/bladder cancer (mostly EFO: urinary bladder carcinoma), with broadly aligned phenotyping (e.g., incident bladder cancer / cancer of bladder / urothelial carcinoma). However, most reported AUROC values appear to be full-model (covariate-influenced) and there are no clearly labeled PGS-only AUROC/R2 values across the set, limiting comparability and making the apparent very high full-model AUROCs (e.g., ~0.80 in PGS000071/PGS000782) non-decisive for PRS quality per the provided domain guidance.

Within the largest coherent study family (Cancer PRSweb; PGS000607–PGS000616), the strongest discrimination signal visible is for the GWAS-hits models, especially PGS000611 (GWAS Hits, 13 variants) with full-model AUROC 0.567 and the strongest top-1% vs rest risk enrichment (OR 2.66, 95% CI 1.79–3.93), plus a moderate per-SD effect size (OR 1.284, 95% CI 1.211–1.361) and basic covariates (age, sex, PCs). Compared with sibling models in the same family, PGS000611’s top-1% enrichment is materially higher than MGI-based GWAS-hits (e.g., PGS000610 top-1% OR 1.47) and higher than P+T/lassosum/PRS-CS siblings, while avoiding reliance on an outlier full-model AUROC.

Main limitation: evidence remains incomplete for a ‘high-quality’ call because key PRS-comparable metrics (PGS-only AUROC or PRS-only R2) are absent across candidates, and several non-PRSweb candidates report only covariate-inclusive full-model metrics or effect sizes with unclear comparability; thus the selection is based on the most internally comparable signals available (within-family effect sizes/top-percentile enrichment + basic covariates) rather than definitive PRS-only performance."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS001262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 19 candidates are direct matches to angina pectoris (trait_efo aligns), but the pool is dominated by (i) UKB pan-trait sparse snpnet models (PGS001260/PGS001261/PGS001262), (ii) UKB biomarker-focused multi-PRS (PGS000703) with only full-model AUROC reported, (iii) 2024 Commun Biol risk-factor integration models (PGS004457/PGS004527) with only ORs and unknown covariates, and (iv) 2024 HGG Adv method-comparison portability models (PGS005047–PGS005059) reporting C-index/OR but with limited training detail (samples_training=N/A) and a benchmarking/portability framing.

Among candidates with explicit PRS-comparable metrics, the snpnet set reports PGS-only AUROC (~0.58) and PGS-only R² (~0.01). Within that family, PGS001262 has the strongest selected European validation full-model AUROC (0.807) and the largest incremental AUROC (0.0098) and PGS-only R² (0.01304), while keeping basic covariates (age, sex, array, PCs). It also has a specific doctor-diagnosed angina phenotype label. The heritability sanity check (h2_obs ~0.009–0.014 depending on endpoint) does not flag the reported PGS-only R² as exceeding plausible ceilings.

Limitation: the selected best option is still from the 'across 813 traits' UKB framework (a known warning sign for generalizability per the provided domain knowledge), and several non-framework candidates (e.g., PGS004527 OR~1.43; multiple PGS0050xx with OR~1.45–1.49 and C-index ~0.765) look competitive but lack directly comparable PRS-only metrics and have incomplete/unclear covariate/training comparability in the visible metadata. This prevents a clearly high-quality, unconflicted choice."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY","best_model_id":"PGS005252","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 returned models, only PGS005252 and PGS005254 are direct phenotype matches for the target trait (trait_reported/trait_efo = aortic stenosis; phenotyping_reported = incident aortic stenosis). PGS005255 and PGS005256 are aortic valve function measures (mean pressure gradient, peak aortic velocity) rather than the disease endpoint, and the remaining models are for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, so they are not direct-match for aortic stenosis.

Between the two incident aortic stenosis models, PGS005252 is better supported by the visible evidence because it provides a discriminative metric (C-index/full-model = 0.87) and a strong time-to-event effect size (HR=1.92) with a large validation sample (n=446,895). PGS005254 reports only an HR (1.64, with CI) and no AUC/C-index/R², providing less directly comparable performance evidence in this context.

Main limitation: the reported C-index for PGS005252 appears to be a full-model metric with substantial clinical covariate adjustment (multiple cardiometabolic comorbidities and BMI/smoking/renal failure), and no PRS-only AUROC/R² is provided, so the PRS-specific contribution cannot be isolated; this lowers confidence to Moderate despite strong endpoint alignment and effect size."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004535",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Both candidates are direct matches to the target trait (ICD10 J33; trait_efo Nasal Cavity Polyp) and come from the same publication, with identical EUR-only development/evaluation ancestry, training cohort (UKB), training size (n=174,489), validation size (n=56,192), and variant count (1,059,939). Neither reports PRS-comparable AUC/R2, so selection must rely on the remaining visible evidence; PGS004535 reports a stronger per-SD odds ratio (OR=1.600668) than PGS004465 (OR=1.3496), which domain guidance treats as a meaningful indicator of stronger PRS discriminative power when AUC/R2 are missing. The main limitation is that covariates are listed as unknown for both and the lack of PRS-only discrimination metrics prevents a high-confidence recommendation; additionally, the method difference (LDpred2 vs RFDiseasemetaPRS) cannot be resolved from the provided metadata alone beyond the observed OR."
}
